  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe primary concern remains dangerous heat through early this\nevening. The Midsouth will remain under the eastern periphery of\na 597dam 500mb ridge, centered over western KS. As we move\nthrough this evening, the ridge axis will drop into northern MS,\nexposing the Midsouth to 30-35kt of deep layer (surface-6km)\nshear. The combination of modest midlevel height falls and the\napproach of a surface cold front will sufficiently erode CINH for\nstorms to develop over the lower OH River Valley. These storms\nwill move southward into the Midsouth, mainly after 21Z / 4 PM\nCDT.  Surface temps in the mid/upper 90s and dewpoints in the\nupper 70s will support impressive mixed-layer CAPE and PWAT\nvalues of 3500 J/kg and 2.3 inches, respectively.\n\nDespite the parameters mentioned above, 12Z CAMs continued to\nshow relatively wide variation in favored corridors for storms\nthis afternoon and evening. 700mb temps are depicted by the RAP\nto remain near 13C into early evening, which is quite warm, even\nby July standards.  In addition, RAP soundings depict relatively\ndry air in the midlevels, above the base of an elevated mixed\nlayer. Potential dry air entrainment by storm updrafts may serve\nto limit storm coverage. Where more persistent and organized\nstorms develop, inverted-V low level sounding profiles and DCAPE\nnearing 1200 J/kg will support a damaging wind threat.\n\nBeyond tonight, drier post-frontal air will settle into the\nMidsouth, with 60s dewpoints moving through all but northeast MS\nWednesday afternoon. As such, heat indices will prevail below\n105 over all but perhaps Monroe County MS. No extension to the\ncurrent heat headlines are planned at this time.\n\nA few storms will be possible over northeast MS, near the stalled\nfront Wednesday night and Thursday. Rain chances may be aided by\nmidlevel moisture left from Tropical Storm Bertha's remnants. For\nthe remainder of the Mid-South, northwest flow aloft will bring a\nchance of thunderstorms on Friday. Thereafter, a southern plains\nupper ridge will expand east, likely necessitating a return of\nheat headlines for the weekend.\n\nPWB",
